g)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a cubic polynomial $f(x).$\nFind $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $-7.$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{3}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-4}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981281/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d429f4182075afe8ff59b1af29627084f4c760cb3c330faa79637b60c63bdd0"} {"question" "q-316704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=1$ and ${x}_{2,3}=\\pm 4\\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=-7.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-7(x-1)(x-4\\text{i})(x-(-4\\text{i})) \\\\  & =-7(x-1)(x-4\\text{i})(x+4\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7(x-1)(x-4\\text{i})(x+4\\text{i}) & = \\\\ -7(x-1)({x}^{2}+x(4\\text{i})-x(4\\text{i})-(4\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ -7(x-1)({x}^{2}+16) & = \\\\ -7({x}^{3}+16x-{x}^{2}-16) & = \\\\ -7{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-112x+112.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-7{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-112x+112.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981281/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d429f4182075afe8ff59b1af29627084f4c760cb3c330faa79637b60c63bdd0"} {"question" "q-316704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "84105d3e3c2789c6fb700d3bfd509a862a2e8fa15a9f73cee39754341118d6c3"} {"question" "q-316704" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacadem